# Line endings, and why this file has to exist.
#
# core.autocrlf=true on Windows checks text files out as CRLF. The skill
# archives under dist/ are built by zipping the working tree, so a Windows
# build embeds CRLF while a Linux CI checkout has LF, and `--verify` then
# reports all 36 text files in the plugins as drifted when nothing changed.
# The build is only reproducible if the working tree is byte-identical on
# every platform.
#
# text=auto rather than text: it lets git's own binary detection skip the
# 11MB PDF and the material-list CSVs. Forcing eol on a PDF corrupts it.
skills/**    text=auto eol=lf
plugins/**   text=auto eol=lf
tools/**     text=auto eol=lf
install.py   text=auto eol=lf

# Built archives. Never diffed, never line-ending converted.
dist/**      binary
*.skill      binary
*.plugin     binary

# The rules above normalise on checkout and on add. They do NOT rewrite a file
# already sitting in the working tree with CRLF, and the archives are zipped
# from the working tree, so a stale file drifts even with this in place. After
# changing these rules, or after any file arrives from a Windows editor, run:
#
#     git add --renormalize .
#
# That is what actually made the working tree match, in 2026-09.

